{
  "id": "Compound::DB08882 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Linagliptin, a Dipeptidyl Peptidase 4 Inhibitor, is linked to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its inclusion in the class of drugs that includes Sitagliptin, which is known to treat this condition. The shared ancestry of Linagliptin and Sitagliptin, both belonging to the Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4 Inhibitor class, suggests a potential mechanism of action that may be relevant to the pathophysiology of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. The evidence from the paths indicates that Linagliptin may exert a therapeutic effect through a mechanism that is not yet fully understood, possibly involving the inhibition of Dipeptidyl Peptidase 4, which is implicated in the regulation of glucose metabolism. The presence of Orlistat, which is a substrate of Dipeptidyl Peptidase 4, in the causal pathway further supports the idea that the inhibition of this enzyme could influence glucose homeostasis. While the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated, the integrated evidence suggests that Linagliptin may have a role in the management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through an indirect pathway involving the Dipeptidyl Peptidase 4 enzyme.",
